AD  - Duke University School of Medicine, Department of Surgery, Durham, NC, United States
AD  - University of Cincinnati College of Medicine, Division of Thoracic Surgery, Cincinnati, OH, United States
AD  - Massachusetts General Hospital, Department of Surgery, Boston, MA, United States
AD  - Indiana University School of Medicine, Division of Thoracic Surgery, Indianapolis, IN, United States
AB  - Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) has been treated with upfront surgical resection in patients who are early-stage and can physiologically tolerate a lobectomy. The current methods of lobectomy include thoracotomy, video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS), and robot-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (RATS). Minimally invasive techniques have rapidly become the standard of care in these settings. Studies have thoroughly investigated and published on the benefits of minimally invasive resections as compared to the open approach. However, direct comparisons between video-assisted versus robot-assisted lobectomy in terms of perioperative outcomes and short-term and long-term oncologic efficacy have been limited. In addition, the patterns of clinical adoption differ between these two minimally invasive surgical techniques. The purpose of this comprehensive review is to discuss the different port placements of video-assisted and robot-assisted lobectomy and compare the robotic platform to standard video-assisted thoracoscopy in the treatment of resectable early-staged NSCLC. We aim to evaluate perioperative morbility, length of stay, and short-term mortality, overall survival, and nodal upstaging. The impact of volume and experiences on thoracoscopy to thoracotomy conversion rates will be addressed. The review will also assess the challending learning curves for both technqies and the current costs incurred by these minimally invasive approaches. The history of pulmonary lobectomy is critical in understanding its current and future evolution. © Video-Assisted Thoracic Surgery.
