TI  - Extended resections for lung cancer with invasion of the chest wall, superior sulcus and spine
ST  - Erweiterte Resektionen beim Lungenkarzinom mit Infiltration von Brustwand, Sulcus superior und Wirbelsäule
PY  - 2014
T2  - Onkologe
VL  - 20
IS  - 10
SP  - 969
EP  - 981
DO  - 10.1007/s00761-014-2733-z
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84911390452&doi=10.1007%2Fs00761-014-2733-z&partnerID=40&md5=04d69987a8b984c45a43b4b6d007a0a0
AD  - Ruhrlandklinik, Abteilung für Thoraxchirurgie und thorakale Endoskopie, Essen, Nordrhein-Westfalen, Germany
AB  - Background: Currently, operative resection is the treatment of choice for early stage lung cancer. Due to the evolution of operative techniques, interdisciplinary teamwork and perioperative management even extended resections of the chest wall, superior sulcus and vertebral bodies can be performed with curative intent.Objectives: This article provides an overview of surgical techniques, operative morbidity and long-term results of cT3 and cT4 tumors involving the chest wall, superior sulcus and vertebral bodies. The overview enables the oncologist to provide qualified patient management.Discussion: Due to the rareness of these T3 and T4 tumor localizations larger randomized studies are not possible; however, in several retrospective series a significant prolongation of survival of R0 resected patients, compared with the R2 resected patients underlines the significance of radical surgery in these cases.Material and methods: The topic is illustrated and evaluated through a current selective literature search complemented by personal clinical experience.Results: Complete resection of cT3 tumors of the chest wall is almost always possible. If the resection defect is larger than 5 cm, alloplastic material for coverage is necessary to prevent paradoxical ventilation. Trimodal treatment with neoadjuvant chemoradiation therapy is established as the standard of care, followed by operative resection whenever possible. Infiltration of the primary tumor into a vertebral body (T4) > 50 % is generally accepted as an inoperability criterion. Lesser infiltration can be resected by experienced thoracic surgeons in cooperation with neurosurgeons or orthopedic spine surgeons with acceptable levels of morbidity. The probability of survival of T3 and T4 non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) with chest wall involvement or vertebral body involvement is 15–50 % at 5 years after radical surgery. © 2014, Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg.
